PDCS installation and tests
===========================

CPU installation (Julia 1.10 or newer)
--------------------------------------
Install Julia from https://julialang.org/downloads/ and make `julia` available
on PATH. In the root of this source checkout, run:

    MODE=cpu bash build.sh

This is the recommended end-to-end CPU build/test entry point. It runs the
bundled example and the following Julia package commands:

    julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'
    julia --project=. examples/soc.jl
    julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'

Pkg downloads the Julia dependencies automatically; the first run needs network
access and may take time to compile packages. Tests print a Test Summary and
exit unsuccessfully if an assertion fails. No external data is needed.
The CPU workflow uses Julia's package manager on Linux, macOS, and Windows;
CI is configured for all three platforms. GPU tests are separate.
The non-stdlib dependencies are JuMP, MathOptInterface, DataStructures, Match,
Polynomials, SnoopPrecompile, Statistics, and PythonCall. CUDA is optional.

To use this checkout from another Julia environment:

    using Pkg
    Pkg.develop(path="/absolute/path/to/PDCS")
    using PDCS: PDCS_CPU

Optional GPU installation
-------------------------
In the Julia environment where you use PDCS, run Pkg.add("CUDA"). You need a
compatible NVIDIA device and driver. Load CUDA before PDCS.PDCS_GPU:

    using CUDA
    using PDCS: PDCS_GPU

Native grid-wise projection also requires GNU make, a CUDA toolkit with nvcc,
and the shared libraries/PTX built from src/pdcs_gpu/cuda. Follow that directory's
README.md to select an architecture, build artifacts, and run GPU regressions.
Do not assume the default sm_90 architecture matches your device.

The end-to-end GPU entry point builds the native projection artifacts and runs
the hardware suite:

    CUDA_HOME=/usr/local/cuda GPU_ARCH=sm_80 MODE=gpu bash build.sh

Use `sm_80` for A100 or `sm_90` for H100, and adjust `CUDA_HOME` to the installed
toolkit. `MODE=all` runs the CPU and GPU paths in one invocation.

PythonCall is installed as a package dependency for the GPU CVXPY bridge.
CPU use does not load PythonCall or require configuring Python. The optional
CVXPY integration requires its own Python/CVXPY environment.

See Project.toml for dependency UUIDs and version constraints, README.md for
the package overview, CONTRIBUTING.md for the test workflow, and RELEASE.md for
the version/tag procedure.
